WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.906
In this lesson, you will learn to use the
Rail.

00:00:04.906 --> 00:00:08.617
It is made for people who run several
coding agents at once,

00:00:08.617 --> 00:00:10.410
such as Claude Code or Codex.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:15.246
Most of your agents are working at any
moment, and only one or two need you.

00:00:15.246 --> 00:00:17.548
Yet each terminal takes the same space.

00:00:17.548 --> 00:00:20.735
The Rail gives the room to the session
that needs you.

00:00:22.106 --> 00:00:25.805
A session is Busy while its agent works
and needs nothing from you.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:30.106
It is Waiting when the agent has stopped
and needs you: it finished,

00:00:30.106 --> 00:00:32.498
it asks permission, or it asks a question.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:34.972
First, add a Rail to a Layout.

00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:38.912
In the full-screen editor, right-click a
Zone, and choose Make Rail.

00:00:39.404 --> 00:00:41.835
A narrow Zone at the side works best.

00:00:41.835 --> 00:00:46.634
The Rail is drawn in orange, with a dashed
outline, and it has no number.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:53.242
Next, open Settings, General, and click
Connect Claude Code, or Connect Codex.

00:00:53.609 --> 00:00:56.527
Zonal adds small hooks to the agent's
settings,

00:00:56.527 --> 00:01:00.376
so the agent can tell Zonal when a session
is Busy or Waiting.

00:01:00.376 --> 00:01:04.287
For Codex, run the hooks command once, and
trust Zonal's hooks.

00:01:05.738 --> 00:01:09.147
Now Snap each terminal window to a Zone of
that Layout.

00:01:09.147 --> 00:01:13.735
Here, the editor is in the first Zone, and
two agents are in the next two.

00:01:13.735 --> 00:01:15.285
The Rail is on the right.

00:01:15.704 --> 00:01:19.163
Watch the first terminal. Its session has
just become Busy.

00:01:22.075 --> 00:01:25.542
After twenty seconds of Busy, its window
moves into the Rail,

00:01:25.542 --> 00:01:27.474
where you can still watch it work.

00:01:27.474 --> 00:01:30.202
The window next to it grows into the free
space.

00:01:30.751 --> 00:01:33.041
Now the agent has stopped, and needs you.

00:01:33.041 --> 00:01:36.952
The session is Waiting, and its window
goes straight back to its Zone.

00:01:37.651 --> 00:01:41.357
It does not take focus, so it never
interrupts what you are typing.

00:01:41.972 --> 00:01:45.345
You can also click a window in the Rail to
bring it back at once,

00:01:45.345 --> 00:01:47.006
whenever you want a closer look.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.072
Some ways to use the Rail.

00:01:50.422 --> 00:01:54.891
With an editor and two agents, the session
that needs you is always the big one.

00:01:54.891 --> 00:01:56.121
You never hunt for it.

00:01:56.471 --> 00:02:01.193
With four agents, the Busy ones share the
Rail in slices, each still readable,

00:02:01.193 --> 00:02:02.283
the oldest on top.

00:02:02.633 --> 00:02:04.532
And it is not only for agents.

00:02:04.532 --> 00:02:09.597
A long build or a test run can use the
Rail too: the guide shows the two lines a

00:02:09.597 --> 00:02:10.420
script needs.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:12.538
To recap.

00:02:12.538 --> 00:02:16.769
Make a Rail in the editor, connect your
agent in Settings, General,

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:18.284
and Snap your terminals.

00:02:18.284 --> 00:02:21.568
Busy sessions move to the Rail after
twenty seconds,

00:02:21.568 --> 00:02:24.031
and come back the moment they need you.

00:02:25.581 --> 00:02:29.348
In the last lesson, you will learn to make
Zonal yours, in Settings.
